WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.140
Hi, my name is Luis Jimenez, and I am the
executive chef at La Loncheria in Brooklyn.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.560
Tortas are a traditional dish from Mexico.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.160
There are different kinds of tortas in Mexico,
depending on the region.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.320
But they're mostly famous in Mexico City.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.920
They're made with bread, beans, protein, sometimes
a little bit of cheese, and that is it.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.760
There's a traditional bread we use called
"telera."

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:36.300
Most of the tortas come from the baguette
you find in boulangeries, but it's a wider

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.770
type of bread than the baguette form.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.290
You can find torta stands all over Mexico
City, but most of the time it's made with

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.360
that style of bread.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.530
You can currently find tortas in New York
City, but we're taking tortas to the next

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.140
level by using our skills and experience.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.030
We have a lamb-belly torta, which is called
"La Niña Popov."

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.829
Lamb is a really special meat.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:03.760
It's tricky sometimes.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.700
We took on the challenge of making it really
nice by braising it over night and marinating

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.850
it for 48 hours prior to cooking.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.750
We mix garbanzo beans, or chickpeas, with
black beans, and that's what we smear on the

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.000
bottom of the bread.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.340
Then we add the roasted lamb belly.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.580
We cook the belly in a barbacoa style.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:29.740
I'm a big fan of the barbacoa from the city
of Hidalgo, which is east of Mexico City.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.970
We add pickled tomatillos and Oaxaca cheese,
or string cheese, on our torta.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.939
Then we serve the sandwich au jus, which is
the lamb stock, or the consommé.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:46.299
People can actually dip the torta into the
jus and make it really nice and juicy.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:52.770
We also have the "Ben Ami," which is a play
on the banh mi: a traditional Vietnamese sandwich

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.550
from Southeast Asia.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:59.870
We take a nice piece of brisket, salt it for
48 hours, and then put a double-rub into the

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.659
brisket and braise it.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.299
We get this really nice, pink color around
the brisket and put the adobo sauce on top.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:12.069
We take two slices of the brisket and then
always the same order of ingredients: A smear

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.579
of mayo on the bread, and then black beans
with avocado leaf on the bottom.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:19.719
The black beans always have to be on the bottom.

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:24.609
And then we add the banh mi ingredients, which
are red cabbage, lots of cilantro, and slices

00:02:24.609 --> 00:02:26.260
of jalapeño.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:35.120
What we really want is to show that tortas
can be made in many different ways.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.360
I have a background in French cooking and
Japanese cooking, and I wanted to bring that

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.360
to La Loncheria.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.430
I wanted to combine those experiences with
the traditional way of making tortas, which

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.590
I grew up eating.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.040
I wanted to combine both elements—the traditional
way of making tortas and my experience—into

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.459
La Loncheria.

